ering heat wave in california  drought is now to blame for not only  wildfires and farming losses but it's  also wiping out fish in record numbers  the situation's bad what's happening in  northern california rivers is the water  is too hot  to allow  adult salmon coming back  to reproduce this year thousands of  salmon are dying as water levels inch  lower and temperatures in rivers around  california continue to rise  we have four  unique runs of chinook salmon that are  native to the sacramento valley  thousands of young salmon have died in  the klamath river as drought conditions  fueled parasite growth and in the  sacramento river wildlife officials are  now saying they are facing a complete  loss of young chinook salmon due to  abnormally warm water the headline is  that  they are very close to extinction not  likely to survive the migration from the  rivers to the ocean on their own the  california department of fish and  wildlife has stepped in loading young  salmon onto trucks and driving hours to  release them into the ocean the solution  is to put them in tanker trucks give  them a ride down to the ocean and let  them go and that's what occurred this  year although the biggest impact has  been on young salmon adult salmon  haven't gone untouched many of the adult  salmon that have come back are already  dying before they can even spawn  of those that are able to spawn their  eggs which are buried in gravel  underneath the river are dying in river  water because the river water is getting  too hot i don't know 13 pound average  maybe for fisherman larry collins what's  happening has a direct impact on his  ability to put food on his table and  yours i got fish that go to farmers  markets restaurants like that the  industry alone in california is worth  1.4 billion dollars with thousands of  salmon now dying fewer will make it to  store shelves down the line driving  prices higher and that sticker shock is  already setting in  the implications of global warming  threatening to disrupt sensitive  ecosystems and wipe out entire salmon  species in california potentially having  far-reaching consequences rob malcolm  global news sacramento  that is global national for this  saturday night i'm robin gill tonight  your canada is a beautiful sunrise taken  from prince edward island national park  we'd love to see your corner of the  country so please keep sending your  favorite photos to viewers at  globalnational.com  thank you for watching and i'll see you  back here tomorrow have a great night  [Music]  you
 

